PRD — Personal Civic Agent for India
1. Problem statement — who hurts and why
Indian citizens often cannot access routine government services without navigating fragmented portals, confusing eligibility rules, document uploads, OTPs, office visits, and unresponsive helplines. The burden falls hardest on senior citizens, students, migrant workers, and people with limited digital literacy or language fluency.

They lose time, wages, benefits, and confidence—or pay informal middlemen—because they cannot reliably complete and follow up on applications themselves.

The product is a consent-first civic agent: citizens explain their need by voice in their language; the agent determines the service and requirements, collects documents, fills real portal forms using computer use, asks for approval before irreversible actions, tracks progress, and provides plain-language updates and escalation support.
2. Target user + 2 personas
Target user: Indian residents who need help completing a government-service request and can access a smartphone and receive OTPs/calls. The initial release targets Hindi and English speakers; other Indian languages follow only after quality validation.

Persona 1 — Savitri, 68, pensioner
- Lives in a tier-2 city; speaks Hindi and has low confidence using websites.
- Needs a pension restart or income certificate.
- Has identity documents as paper copies or WhatsApp photos.
- Wants to understand what is happening without sharing control of her identity or bank details.
- Success: “My application was submitted, I got the receipt, and someone tells me the next step in Hindi.”
Persona 2 — Imran, 24, migrant worker/student
- Moves between states for work or study; primarily uses an Android phone.
- Needs a domicile/income certificate or scholarship application.
- Risks missing deadlines because documents and portals differ by location.
- Wants a fast, trustworthy alternative to a broker.
- Success: “I completed the process without taking a day off work or paying someone unofficially.”
3. Goals and non-goals
Goals
- Convert a spoken, vague request into a specific government-service application.
- Complete one supported service on a real portal and obtain an acknowledgement number.
- Collect and validate required documents with explicit, granular consent.
- Use computer automation to fill forms while preserving citizen control.
- Pause before each irreversible action: submission, payment, e-sign, declaration, and outbound call.
- Track submitted applications and explain progress in the citizen’s language.
- Create an auditable record of consent, actions, data access, and portal outcomes.
Non-goals
- Supporting every government service, state, portal, or document in the MVP.
- Bypassing CAPTCHAs, OTPs, e-signature, biometric requirements, portal terms, or human verification.
- Making eligibility decisions, fabricating documents, or representing itself as the citizen or a government official.
- Holding funds, paying fees autonomously, or accessing bank credentials.
- Guaranteeing approval, processing time, or an officer response.
- Replacing legal aid, grievance redressal, or human review for complex cases.
4. User stories
- As a citizen, I want to describe my need by voice in Hindi or English so that I do not need to understand a government portal.
- As a citizen, I want the agent to ask only the questions needed for my service so that I can finish intake quickly.
- As a citizen, I want to see why each document is required and approve its use so that I remain in control of my identity data.
- As a citizen, I want the agent to tell me what is missing or unreadable so that I can correct it before submission.
- As a citizen, I want the agent to fill the portal form for me so that I avoid errors and repetitive typing.
- As a citizen, I want to review a plain-language summary before submission so that I can catch mistakes.
- As a citizen, I want to enter my own OTP and approve declarations so that the agent cannot act beyond my permission.
- As a citizen, I want a receipt and acknowledgement number after submission so that I have proof of my application.
- As a citizen, I want periodic status updates in my preferred language so that I know whether action is needed.
- As a citizen, I want the agent to prepare and, after approval, place a transparent follow-up call so that I do not have to navigate inaccessible helplines.
- As a support operator, I want to review failed or uncertain automation runs without seeing unnecessary identity data so that I can help safely.
5. Feature list split into MVP / v2 / later
MVP
1. Voice-led service intake in Hindi and English.
2. Narrow service catalog for one state and one to two services selected before build.
3. Eligibility and document checklist engine for supported services.
4. Secure document capture, document-quality checks, and citizen consent controls.
5. Real-portal computer-use workflow for supported services.
6. Citizen confirmation gates for OTP, declaration, payment, e-sign, submission, and phone calls.
7. Submission receipt capture and acknowledgement-number extraction.
8. Application status checks and plain-language updates.
9. Stalled-application detection plus approved voice-call workflow/script.
10. Consent, action, and data-access audit trail.
V2
- Additional Indian languages based on measured speech-recognition quality.
- Additional states and service workflows.
- Assisted document extraction and prefill from user-approved documents.
- Optional caregiver/community-worker delegation with separate permissions.
- Appointment booking and office-visit preparation.
- Grievance/escalation draft generation for eligible cases.
- Human support handoff for low-confidence cases.
- WhatsApp/SMS updates where legally and operationally viable.
Later
- Broad multi-state service marketplace.
- Verified partner network for assisted service centers.
- Proactive benefit discovery.
- Cross-portal reusable citizen profile, subject to consent and legal review.
- Full case-management integrations with government departments.
- Payment orchestration through compliant third-party payment providers.
6. Detailed functional requirements per MVP feature
A. Voice-led intake
- Support Hindi and English voice input; display the transcript and allow correction before proceeding.
- Ask for preferred language, location/state, service need, urgency, and whether a caregiver is assisting.
- Convert vague requests into one of: supported service, unsupported service, or insufficient information.
- Explain the proposed service in plain language and require citizen confirmation before document collection.
- Store the original audio only with explicit consent; default to storing transcript and structured answers instead.
- Escalate to human support or provide a referral when confidence is below the defined threshold.
B. Service catalog and requirements engine
- Each supported service must define: state, portal URL, eligibility criteria, required/optional documents, fee, SLA if published, form fields, OTP/e-sign rules, status-check method, and escalation path.
- The agent may only initiate an application when the service configuration is approved and current.
- The system must show the citizen a checklist with “required,” “optional,” and “not needed” categories.
- Any uncertain interpretation of rules must block autonomous submission and request user confirmation or human review.
C. Document collection and validation
- Accept camera scans, image uploads, and PDFs.
- Classify document type only after citizen identifies or confirms it.
- Check legibility, all pages, expiry where relevant, name/date mismatches, and missing fields.
- Never alter, generate, or “repair” a government document.
- Explain mismatches in plain language and suggest acceptable alternatives from the service configuration.
- Encrypt documents at rest and in transit; apply per-application access control.
- Provide a visible “delete application data” option, subject to retention obligations and already-submitted portal data.
D. Consent and identity-data controls
- Obtain separate affirmative consent for: document processing, portal access, each document upload, submission, outbound calling, and optional audio retention.
- Before portal automation begins, show the portal name, purpose, and fields/documents to be used.
- Before irreversible actions, show a concise review screen and require active confirmation.
- OTPs must be entered by the citizen or passed through an ephemeral secure handoff; never log, reuse, or expose them to operators.
- The agent must not store Aadhaar numbers, PAN, bank details, passwords, or OTPs unless a documented legal/compliance review explicitly authorizes a narrowly scoped exception. Default design: redact/minimize these fields.
- Every consent must be timestamped, versioned, revocable for future actions, and linked to the requested purpose.
E. Real-portal computer use
- Run portal interaction in an isolated browser session per application.
- Navigate only allowlisted government domains configured for the service.
- Fill fields from citizen-confirmed data; never infer sensitive facts such as caste, income, disability, or marital status.
- Capture step-level evidence: timestamp, action type, portal screen state, success/failure, and redacted screenshot where permitted.
- Stop and ask the citizen to act for CAPTCHA, OTP, e-sign, biometric verification, account credentials, or any portal message outside expected flow.
- Detect broken pages, session expiry, changed form labels, upload failures, and unexpected redirects; stop instead of guessing.
- On success, capture the acknowledgement number, receipt, submission timestamp, and next expected action.
F. Submission review and confirmation
- Present a readable summary of submitted answers, attachments, fees, declarations, and portal destination.
- Require explicit confirmation for each submission attempt, including retries after failure.
- If a fee is required, redirect the citizen to the authorized payment page; the product must not receive or retain payment credentials.
- Preserve the receipt after submission and make it accessible in the citizen’s preferred language.
G. Status tracking and citizen updates
- Check application status only at configured intervals and only while status-tracking consent remains active.
- Parse the official portal’s status text, retain the original status, and provide a plain-language explanation.
- Notify the citizen when: status changes, action is required, a deadline approaches, the expected SLA is exceeded, or tracking fails repeatedly.
- Show the latest official source, check timestamp, acknowledgement number, and recommended next action.
H. Stalled-case follow-up and voice calling
- Define “stalled” per service: no movement beyond the published SLA, or no change for a configured number of days when no SLA exists.
- Generate a call script containing only necessary case details: citizen-approved identity verification details, application number, submission date, and requested status.
- Show the script and require approval immediately before calling.
- At call start, disclose that the caller is an automated assistant calling on behalf of the citizen, where permitted.
- Record calls only with legally valid notice and consent; otherwise retain a structured call outcome and minimal notes.
- The voice agent must not threaten, bribe, misrepresent authority, reveal unnecessary documents, or make legal claims.
- If the officer asks for a prohibited action or unofficial payment, end the call and flag the case to the citizen.
I. Audit trail and user control
- Maintain immutable event records for consent, data access, document upload, automation steps, confirmations, status checks, call outcomes, and deletion requests.
- Allow the citizen to view: what was shared, with whom, why, and when.
- Allow the citizen to pause tracking, withdraw future consent, revoke delegation, and request data deletion.
- Support internal support access through role-based, time-bound permissions with audit logging.
